This error is a classic "duplicate" issue. Spring is refusing to start because you have two different methods in your GlobalExceptionHandler trying to handle the same exception: DataAccessException.
Specifically, the conflict is between:
 * handleDatabaseSecurity (the one we just added for security)
 * handleDbError (the one you already had in your file)
Spring doesn't know which one to choose when a database error occurs, so it crashes during startup. To fix this, you need to merge them into a single method.
The Fix: Merged & Hardened GlobalExceptionHandler
Go to your GlobalExceptionHandler.java file, delete both handleDbError and handleDatabaseSecurity, and replace them with this single, production-ready method:
/**
 * Rule 2 & 3: Consolidated Database Exception Handler.
 * Fixes the 'Ambiguous @ExceptionHandler' startup error.
 * This method replaces both handleDbError and handleDatabaseSecurity.
 */
@ExceptionHandler(org.springframework.dao.DataAccessException.class)
public ResponseEntity<ApiErrorResponseDto> handleDatabaseOperation(
        org.springframework.dao.DataAccessException ex, 
        jakarta.servlet.http.HttpServletRequest request) {
    
    // 1. Identify the root cause (The actual ORA- error from Oracle)
    Throwable rootCause = org.springframework.core.NestedExceptionUtils.getMostSpecificCause(ex);
    String errorCode = "DB_TECHNICAL_ERROR";
    
    if (rootCause instanceof java.sql.SQLException) {
        // Rule 2: Log only the Error Code to prevent PII/Schema leakage
        errorCode = "ORA-" + ((java.sql.SQLException) rootCause).getErrorCode();
    }

    // Rule 2: Masked logging for production security
    log.error("DATABASE_FAULT | Path: {} | ErrorCode: {} | Status: Masked for PII Protection", 
              request.getRequestURI(), errorCode);

    // 2. Return a sanitized response to the user/frontend
    return buildResponse(
        HttpStatus.INTERNAL_SERVER_ERROR, 
        "Database Error", 
        "A technical error occurred in the data layer. Reference: " + errorCode, 
        request.getRequestURI(), 
        null
    );
}

Why this fixes the problem:
 * No Ambiguity: Now there is only one method mapped to DataAccessException, so Spring can safely instantiate the HandlerExceptionResolver.
 * Keeps your Logic: It still uses your existing buildResponse helper and ApiErrorResponseDto.
 * Hardened Security: It keeps the security improvement we discussed—capturing the Oracle error code (like ORA-20001) for debugging while keeping the actual sensitive error message out of the logs.
Final Checklist:
 * Open GlobalExceptionHandler.java.
 * Search for @ExceptionHandler(org.springframework.dao.DataAccessException.class).
 * Ensure there is only one method with that annotation.
 * Save and re-run.
This should get your ApplicationContext up and running immediately. Ready to move forward with the final security check?
